::ALG2_U01_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 8x<sup>3</sup> - 12x<sup>2</sup>. {
=4x<sup>2</sup>(2x - 3) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 4x squared.
~4x(2x<sup>2</sup> - 3x) #This factors out 4x but not the greatest common factor.
~2x<sup>2</sup>(4x - 6) #This is not fully factored because 4x - 6 still has a common factor of 2.
~4x<sup>2</sup>(2x + 3) #The sign inside the factor should be negative.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 12x<sup>2</sup> + 18x. {
~3x(4x + 6) #This is not fully factored because 4x + 6 still has a common factor of 2.
=6x(2x + 3) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 6x.
~6x(2x - 3) #The sign should be positive because both original terms are positive.
~x(12x + 18) #This misses the numerical greatest common factor.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 64. {
=(x - 8)(x + 8) #Correct. This is x squared minus 8 squared.
~(x - 8)(x - 8) #This expands to x squared - 16x + 64.
~(x + 8)(x + 8) #This expands to x squared + 16x + 64.
~x(x - 64) #This expands to x squared - 64x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 4x<sup>2</sup> - 25. {
~(2x - 5)(2x - 5) #This expands to 4x squared - 20x + 25.
=(2x - 5)(2x + 5) #Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~(4x - 5)(x + 5) #This does not multiply back to 4x squared - 25.
~(2x + 5)(2x + 5) #This expands to 4x squared + 20x + 25.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> + 10x + 21. {
~(x + 1)(x + 21) #This gives a middle term of 22x.
=(x + 3)(x + 7) #Correct. 3 and 7 multiply to 21 and add to 10.
~(x - 3)(x - 7) #This gives a negative middle term.
~(x + 4)(x + 6) #This multiplies to 24, not 21.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 11x + 24. {
~(x + 3)(x + 8) #This gives a positive middle term.
=(x - 3)(x - 8) #Correct. -3 and -8 multiply to 24 and add to -11.
~(x - 4)(x - 6) #This gives a middle term of -10x.
~(x + 4)(x + 6) #This gives a positive middle term and the wrong middle coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 28. {
=(x - 7)(x + 4) #Correct. -7 and 4 multiply to -28 and add to -3.
~(x + 7)(x - 4) #This gives a middle term of 3x.
~(x - 14)(x + 2) #This gives a middle term of -12x.
~(x - 7)(x - 4) #This gives a positive constant.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 35. {
~(x + 5)(x - 7) #This gives a middle term of -2x.
=(x + 7)(x - 5) #Correct. 7 and -5 multiply to -35 and add to 2.
~(x - 7)(x - 5) #This gives a positive constant.
~(x + 1)(x - 35) #This gives the wrong middle term.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> + 9x + 20. {
~(x + 2)(x + 10) #This gives a middle term of 12x.
=(x + 4)(x + 5) #Correct. 4 and 5 multiply to 20 and add to 9.
~(x - 4)(x - 5) #This gives a negative middle term.
~(x + 1)(x + 20) #This gives a middle term of 21x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 12x + 36. {
~(x + 6)(x + 6) #This gives a positive middle term.
=(x - 6)(x - 6) #Correct. -6 and -6 multiply to 36 and add to -12.
~(x - 4)(x - 9) #This multiplies to 36 but gives a middle term of -13x.
~(x + 4)(x + 9) #This gives a positive middle term.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is a difference of squares? {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 16 #This is a sum of squares, not a difference.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 16 #Correct. This is x squared minus 4 squared.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 10x + 25 #This is a perfect square trinomial.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 16 #This is a perfect square trinomial.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is fully factored? {
~2(x<sup>2</sup> - 9) #The binomial is still a difference of squares.
=2(x - 3)(x + 3) #Correct. This is fully factored over the real numbers.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 9 #This expression is not factored.
~(2x - 6)(x + 3) #The first factor still has a common factor of 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to (x + 4)(x + 6)? {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 10 #This misses the middle terms and has the wrong constant.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 10x + 24 #Correct. Multiplying gives x squared + 6x + 4x + 24.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 24x + 10 #The middle coefficient and constant are switched.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 10x + 24 #The middle term sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which factored form is equivalent to x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 6? {
~(x + 2)(x + 3) #This gives a positive middle term.
=(x - 2)(x - 3) #Correct. -2 and -3 multiply to 6 and add to -5.
~(x - 1)(x - 6) #This gives a middle term of -7x.
~(x + 1)(x + 6) #This gives a positive middle term.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which factored form is equivalent to x<sup>2</sup> + x - 30? {
~(x + 5)(x - 6) #This gives a middle term of -x.
=(x + 6)(x - 5) #Correct. 6 and -5 multiply to -30 and add to 1.
~(x - 6)(x - 5) #This gives a positive constant.
~(x + 3)(x - 10) #This gives a middle term of -7x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the greatest common factor of 15x<sup>3</sup> and 10x<sup>2</sup>? {
~5x #This is a common factor, but it is not the greatest common factor.
=5x<sup>2</sup> #Correct. The coefficient GCF is 5 and the smallest power of x is x squared.
~10x<sup>2</sup> #10 is not a factor of 15.
~25x<sup>5</sup> #This multiplies instead of finding a common factor.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 15x<sup>3</sup> - 10x<sup>2</sup>. {
~5x(3x<sup>2</sup> - 2x) #This is not fully factored because x is still common inside.
=5x<sup>2</sup>(3x - 2) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 5x squared.
~10x<sup>2</sup>(5x - 1) #10x squared is not a factor of both terms.
~5x<sup>2</sup>(3x + 2) #The sign inside should be negative.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 2x<sup>2</sup> - 18 completely. {
~2(x<sup>2</sup> - 9) #This is not complete because x squared minus 9 is a difference of squares.
=2(x - 3)(x + 3) #Correct. First factor out 2, then factor the difference of squares.
~(2x - 3)(x + 3) #This does not multiply back to 2x squared - 18.
~2(x - 9)(x + 9) #This would multiply to 2x squared - 162.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 3x<sup>3</sup> - 12x completely. {
=3x(x - 2)(x + 2) #Correct. Factor out 3x, then factor x squared minus 4.
~3x(x<sup>2</sup> - 4) #This is not complete because x squared minus 4 is a difference of squares.
~3(x - 2)(x + 2) #This is missing the factor x.
~x(3x - 12) #This is not fully factored and does not preserve the cubic term correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains why (x - 4)(x + 4) is equivalent to x<sup>2</sup> - 16? {
~The constants add to 0, so the expression equals x squared. #This ignores the constant product.
=Multiplying gives x<sup>2</sup> + 4x - 4x - 16, and the middle terms cancel. #Correct. The product simplifies to x squared - 16.
~The expression is equivalent because 4 plus 4 equals 8. #The sum of the constants does not verify the product.
~It is equivalent only when x equals 0. #Equivalent expressions match for every allowed input, not just one value.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>A student factors x<sup>2</sup> + 11x + 24 as (x + 4)(x + 6). What is the best correction? {
~The factorization is correct because 4 times 6 is 24. #The factors must also add to the middle coefficient.
=Use (x + 3)(x + 8), because 3 and 8 multiply to 24 and add to 11. #Correct. Both the product and sum conditions must be met.
~Use (x - 3)(x - 8), because 3 and 8 multiply to 24. #This gives a negative middle term.
~Use (x + 2)(x + 12), because 2 and 12 multiply to 24. #This gives a middle term of 14x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>A student says x<sup>2</sup> + 25 factors as (x + 5)(x - 5). What is the best correction over the real numbers? {
~The student is correct because 25 is a square. #The expression is a sum of squares, not a difference of squares.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 25 does not factor over the real numbers using this pattern. #Correct. Difference of squares requires subtraction.
~The correct factorization is (x + 5)(x + 5). #This expands to x squared + 10x + 25.
~The correct factorization is x(x + 25). #This expands to x squared + 25x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor pair should be used to factor x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 15? {
~3 and 5 #These multiply to 15 but add to positive 8.
=-3 and -5 #Correct. -3 and -5 multiply to 15 and add to -8.
~-1 and -15 #These multiply to 15 but add to -16.
~1 and 15 #These multiply to 15 but add to 16.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor pair should be used to factor x<sup>2</sup> + 4x - 21? {
~-7 and 3 #These multiply to -21 but add to -4, so the middle term would have the wrong sign.
=7 and -3 #Correct. 7 and -3 multiply to -21 and add to 4.
~3 and -8 #These add to -5 and multiply to -24, so they do not match the trinomial.
~-1 and 21 #These multiply to -21 but add to 20.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is the complete factorization of 4x<sup>3</sup> - 36x? {
~4x(x<sup>2</sup> - 9) #This is not complete because x squared minus 9 still factors.
=4x(x - 3)(x + 3) #Correct. Factor out 4x, then use difference of squares.
~4(x - 3)(x + 3) #This is missing the factor x.
~x(4x - 36) #This is not fully factored and does not preserve the cubic structure.
}
